  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Which of the following are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n\n- $A⊄B$\n- $|A|\\ne |B|$\n- $A⊇B$",
  "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- The relation \"$⊄$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since ${2,3}⊄{2}$ but ${2}⊂{2,3}.$\n\n- The relation \"$\\ne $\" for the cardinalities of sets is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive since $|{2}|=|{2}|.$\n\n- The relation \"$⊇$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since $N⊇{1}$ but ${1}⊉N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
